over the past two months russia has  amassed more than 100 000 troops on the  ukrainian border and everyone is waiting  to see if president vladimir putin will  attempt an invasion but there's another  reason to pay close attention to this  area it's one of the most important  wheat-producing regions on the planet  and no matter where you are in the world  wheat is everywhere including in the  things we buy every single week so what  could an escalation in ukraine mean for  the global grain market and all of us  consumers let's go find out  okay let's go back to that green map for  a second  look at these shaded regions that show  the wheat producing land in russia and  ukraine the greener the shade the more  wheat that's produced as you can see  this area is extremely productive  i mean there's a reason why soviet  symbols and flags and art featured  images of wheat  the land's mineral-rich black soil and  mild conditions make it perfect for  growing grains although the soviet union  system of collective farming was  inefficient and led to shortfalls things  have changed a lot in recent decades  with a shift toward more market-driven  economies russia and ukraine have  unleashed their agricultural power  together the two countries account for  about one-third of global wheat exports  and top importers include egypt turkey  indonesia and pakistan according to the  u.s department of agriculture ukraine is  also a top exporter of other important  staples like corn and barley and most of  those products travel to other countries  by ship via the black sea so now that  you know just how much wheat these  countries grow let's talk about food  prices even just the threat of conflict  has driven wheat prices higher and  traders are watching the region closely  so is wsj commodities reporter will  horner if wheat prices rise by what some  analysts are suggesting then yeah that  means wheat prices everywhere going up  it potentially means food prices  everywhere rising at this point no one  knows how things will play out but  because ukraine's most productive wheat  regions are close to russia and areas  controlled by russian-led separatists  analysts are pricing out a few different  possibilities if russia launches an  all-out invasion of ukraine a worst case  scenario that russia has denied planning  for some say prices could easily double  that's because ukraine's production  could be disrupted and at the same time  russian exports could be sanctioned but  some analysts say even a smaller scale  incursion could push prices 10 to 25  percent higher anything that affects  ukraine's agricultural infrastructure  and that certainly includes ports  is likely to have a big you know impact  on prices ukraine already lost territory  and ports back when russia annexed  crimea in 2014 and many fear that  president putin could attempt a similar  invasion for example russia could make a  play for this territory which putin has  referred to by its tsarist-era term  novorossia or new russia some of it is  already controlled by russian-led  separatists and if russia sought to  expand here it would choke ukraine's  economy by cutting it off from those key  black sea trade routes obviously there  is a lot at stake here for ukraine but  what could a conflict mean for the rest  of the world in terms of wheat global  food prices have already surged and  they're adding to the economic hardship  that many people face due to the  pandemic especially in developing  countries andre seizov a black sea  grains analyst said a further spike in  prices could make matters much worse for  everyone it obviously will be negative  for for the whole world  prices will go everywhere and for all  consumers but obviously the poorer the  countries the bigger will be  the heat to the country and higher food  prices could even lead to unrest in some  parts of the world for example in 2011  we saw that arab spring after in 2010  russia banned its wheat exports if we  see a serious disruption this season and  prices will go up by 20 30 or even  higher these countries mean the  countries that will be a very big  problem for them food prices aren't the  only thing at stake if a conflict breaks  out in ukraine we could see huge global  disruptions when it comes to energy and  metals and even cyber security my  colleagues at the wall street journal  are covering all the developments over  in ukraine so check out wsj.com for the  latest and if you learned something from  this video be sure to subscribe to the  channel for more stories like this one  i'll see you next time
 

